=== runs/a-tcpip-ports.log ===
Walkthrough transcript: a-tcpip-ports (TCP/IP and ports)
Date: 2026-10-09 (UTC) · Environment: Ubuntu container, loopback only
Tool versions: Python 3.12.3; ss (iproute2 6.1.0); nc present; no tcpdump on this machine
Note: this shell runs as root; the privileged-port step is demonstrated by dropping to the 'nobody' user.
Note: this sandbox blocks UDP loopback, so the UDP datagram step is marked not-run (the TCP form of DNS
in the a-dns-depth walkthrough is the same idea over a different transport).

--- step 1: a port is a rendezvous point — open a listener ---
$ ss -tln | grep 18443
LISTEN 0      0          127.0.0.1:18443      0.0.0.0:*          
(LISTEN: the kernel holds this port. A SYN arriving here gets a SYN-ACK back —
 the first two steps of the handshake happen before any application code runs.)

--- step 2: connect — the handshake, observed through socket states ---
$ python3 -c "import socket,time; s=socket.create_connection(('127.0.0.1',18443)); s.recv(20); time.sleep(6)" &
$ ss -tn | grep 18443
ESTAB 0      0          127.0.0.1:37494    127.0.0.1:18443       
ESTAB 0      0          127.0.0.1:18443    127.0.0.1:37494       
(two ESTABLISHED rows: the client got an ephemeral port (see step 4), the server side sits on 18443.
 The SYN / SYN-ACK / ACK exchange completed before create_connection() returned.)

--- step 3: one port, one owner — bind it twice ---
$ python3 -c "import socket; s=socket.socket(); s.bind(('127.0.0.1',18443))"
OSError: [Errno 98] Address already in use
(the kernel refuses a second listener on the same address+port: one owner per port.)

--- step 4: privileged ports — below 1024 needs privilege ---
$ setpriv --reuid=nobody --regid=nogroup --clear-groups python3 -c "import socket; s=socket.socket(); s.bind(('127.0.0.1',80))"
PermissionError: [Errno 13] Permission denied
(PermissionError for an unprivileged user: historically why web servers started as root to bind 80/443;
 modern practice is capabilities, authbind, or a reverse proxy. Ephemeral port range here:)
$ cat /proc/sys/net/ipv4/ip_local_port_range
32768	60999
(client-side ports in step 2 came from this range — the kernel picks them.)

--- step 5: UDP datagrams — not run by us ---
NOT RUN BY US on this machine: the sandbox blocks UDP loopback (PermissionError on sendto).
The concept, to run on your own machine:
  server: python3 -c "import socket; s=socket.socket(socket.AF_INET,socket.SOCK_DGRAM); s.bind(('127.0.0.1',19443)); print(s.recvfrom(1024))"
  client: python3 -c "import socket; s=socket.socket(socket.AF_INET,socket.SOCK_DGRAM); s.sendto(b'ping',('127.0.0.1',19443))"
No handshake, no connection state — ss shows no ESTABLISHED for UDP. DNS uses this (UDP first, TCP fallback).

--- step 6: closing — FIN, half-close states, then TIME_WAIT ---
$ python3 -c "import socket; s=socket.create_connection(('127.0.0.1',18443)); s.recv(20); s.close()" & sleep 2; ss -tn | grep 18443
FIN-WAIT-2 0      0          127.0.0.1:34736    127.0.0.1:18443
FIN-WAIT-2 0      0          127.0.0.1:37494    127.0.0.1:18443
CLOSE-WAIT 1      0          127.0.0.1:18443    127.0.0.1:37494
CLOSE-WAIT 1      0          127.0.0.1:18443    127.0.0.1:34736
(The client sent FIN ("I'm done sending"); our toy server holds its side open, so the close
 stalls half-done: FIN-WAIT-2 on the client, CLOSE-WAIT on the server. A close is a
 negotiation — both sides must FIN before the connection is truly gone.)
$ (server that closes immediately + client that closes at once) ; ss -tna | grep 18445
TIME-WAIT 0      0          127.0.0.1:57402    127.0.0.1:18445
(When both sides FIN, the side that closed first parks in TIME_WAIT — here the client's
 ephemeral port 57402 — so late duplicate packets cannot corrupt the *next* connection
 that reuses the port. tcp_fin_timeout on this machine: 60s. This lingering state is one
 reason a restarted server briefly reports "address already in use" without SO_REUSEADDR.)

--- step 7: the port registry — what the numbers mean ---
$ grep -E '^(http|https|domain|ssh)[[:space:]]' /etc/services
ssh		22/tcp				# SSH Remote Login Protocol
domain		53/tcp				# Domain Name Server
domain		53/udp
http		80/tcp		www		# WorldWideWeb HTTP
https		443/tcp				# http protocol over TLS/SSL
https		443/udp				# HTTP/3
(well-known assignments are convention, not enforcement — anything may listen on any unprivileged port.)

--- step 8: packet capture — not run by us ---
NOT RUN BY US: no tcpdump/Wireshark on this machine.
On your own machine: sudo tcpdump -i lo -n 'tcp port 18443' while repeating step 2 —
read the SYN (flags S), SYN-ACK (flags SA), ACK, data, then FIN exchanges, and match each to the states above.

